\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The square criterion and its hypotheses}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{The square in \ref{fdip-00UX} uses an actual perfect
alternating form and an alternating kernel form. If either
is absent, the paired determinant formula of \ref{fdip-00TL}
remains defined, while this Pfaffian quotient is unavailable.
A square can be zero. The identity alone establishes neither
an inverse kernel nor reflection positivity, and does not
replace the paired coefficient by an unpaired field model.}

\p{Over a field, TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{Matrix.isSquare_det_of_transpose_eq_neg_of_diag_eq_zero}
proves that a skew matrix with zero diagonal has square
determinant, including characteristic #{2}. Its
\leanref/tauceti{Matrix.isSquare_det_of_transpose_eq_neg}
gives the integer-matrix version, where zero diagonal follows
from skew symmetry. These are determinant components. They
neither supply the form #{B} for a coupled kernel nor define
the basis-independent quotient of \ref{fdip-00UX} over an
arbitrary ring.}

\p{The scalar quotient above uses a finite free module.
For a projective module without a chosen global basis, a
corresponding construction would require determinant and
Pfaffian lines; that extension is not part of the scalar
statement.}
